WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.905
In this lesson, you will see which Layout
fits which way of working,

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:06.996
with real examples.

00:00:07.346 --> 00:00:10.905
The best Layout follows two things: the
shape of your display,

00:00:10.905 --> 00:00:12.168
and what you do on it.

00:00:12.168 --> 00:00:15.095
A Layout that matches your work beats a
clever one.

00:00:16.486 --> 00:00:21.306
On a 13 to 16 inch laptop, two windows is
the most that stays readable.

00:00:21.306 --> 00:00:24.836
2 Columns: work on the left, reference on
the right.

00:00:25.526 --> 00:00:29.760
For example, code and its documentation,
an email and the calendar,

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.466
or a draft and its sources.

00:00:32.156 --> 00:00:36.223
On a wide display, the far edges are
uncomfortable to read for long.

00:00:36.223 --> 00:00:40.231
Priority Grid keeps your main window in
the center, straight ahead.

00:00:40.689 --> 00:00:44.961
Chat, music or a terminal go to the narrow
sides, where a glance is enough.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:50.390
On a 27 inch or 4K display, 3 Columns
gives you three equal windows,

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.560
for work that compares things.

00:00:52.973 --> 00:00:57.191
A design, its spec and the build. Or a
browser, an editor and a preview.

00:00:57.747 --> 00:01:02.710
For a video call, a 2 by 2 Grid shows four
windows you watch more than you type in.

00:01:03.068 --> 00:01:07.315
The call, its notes, the shared document
and the chat. Or four dashboards.

00:01:07.915 --> 00:01:10.654
Give one display two Layouts for two
modes,

00:01:10.654 --> 00:01:15.304
for example 2 Columns for focused work,
and the 2 by 2 Grid for meetings.

00:01:15.937 --> 00:01:20.351
Control, Option, Shift and the backtick
key switch to the next Layout.

00:01:20.351 --> 00:01:23.505
Nothing jumps: your next Snap uses the new
Layout.

00:01:25.125 --> 00:01:26.703
Three people, three setups.

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:31.848
A developer: the editor in the middle of a
Priority Grid, the browser on one side,

00:01:31.848 --> 00:01:33.252
a terminal on the other.

00:01:33.602 --> 00:01:37.986
A writer: 3 Columns, with sources, the
draft and notes side by side.

00:01:38.336 --> 00:01:41.692
A manager: a 2 by 2 Grid for calls and
dashboards,

00:01:41.692 --> 00:01:43.774
and 2 Columns between meetings.

00:01:45.324 --> 00:01:45.963
To recap.

00:01:45.963 --> 00:01:51.571
2 Columns on a laptop, Priority Grid on a
wide display, 3 Columns on a big one,

00:01:51.571 --> 00:01:53.772
and a 2 by 2 Grid for meetings.

00:01:53.772 --> 00:01:57.393
Two Layouts give you two modes, one
shortcut apart.

00:01:58.943 --> 00:02:03.304
In the next lesson, you will learn to use
the Rail, if you work with coding agents.
